  "answer_text": "Here is a comprehensive overview of **examples of preventive medicine practices**, organized by type:\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udd35 What Is Preventive Medicine?\n\nPreventive medicine is a holistic approach to healthcare that considers the whole patient and the many factors that influence their well-being. This medical specialty differs from traditional medicine in that its focus is **proactive rather than reactive**.\n\nThere are **3 main types** of preventive medicine: primary, secondary, and tertiary.\n\n---\n\n## 1\ufe0f\u20e3 Primary Prevention\n*(Preventing disease before it occurs)*\n\nPrimary prevention can be defined as an action or behavior that does not allow a disease or adverse event to occur. Examples include **immunization, smoking cessation, or initiation of an exercise program** with the goal of disease prevention.\n\nKey examples include:\n\n- **Vaccinations:** Vaccinations represent perhaps the single most effective preventive care measure in medical history \u2014 they have eradicated or dramatically reduced many diseases that once caused widespread suffering and death. Vaccines are important in building a child's immune system, and children must have shots against diseases like mumps and polio.\n\n- **Lifestyle Counseling:** Clinical preventive medicine physicians may provide counseling for unhealthy habits, run preventive health screenings, and administer immunizations. They may work with patients who would benefit from lifestyle changes, often encountering cases involving diabetes, smoking, or obesity.\n\n- **STI Prevention & Screening:** Anyone who is sexually active should be periodically screened for STIs to protect their health and the health of their partner.\n\n---\n\n## 2\ufe0f\u20e3 Secondary Prevention\n*(Early detection of disease)*\n\nSecondary preventive medicine focuses on the early detection of illness. This gives the patient a better chance of getting treatment, as detecting the ailment early slows down its progression.\n\nKey examples include:\n\n- **Cancer Screenings:** Advances in cancer detection led to improved screening for various malignancies, including the widespread use of **colonoscopy** in colorectal cancer screening, **mammography** in breast cancer screening, and **Pap smear and HPV testing** in cervical cancer.\n\n- **Cardiovascular Screenings:** Screenings for **elevated cholesterol levels** and **hypertension** serve critical roles in preventing, identifying, and controlling cardiovascular diseases.\n\n- **Newborn Screening:** Examples of treatments under secondary prevention also include **newborn screening** and cancer screening.\n\n- **Blood Pressure & Smoking Screenings:** Among the most commonly reported preventive practices by primary care physicians are **blood pressure measurement** (99%), **screening for smoking** (95%), and brief advice for smokers (95%).\n\n---\n\n## 3\ufe0f\u20e3 Tertiary Prevention\n*(Limiting damage from existing disease)*\n\nTertiary preventive medicine aims to **limit the damage** of a current illness. Making the patient as comfortable as possible is part of this level of care. Future care and long-term treatment modalities can help the patient achieve health stability, helping them return to daily routines as quickly as possible.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udf10 Non-Clinical Preventive Medicine\n\nNon-clinical preventive medicine physicians don't work as closely with individual patients. This branch of medicine includes **health policy, epidemiology**, and an increased focus on the social and behavioral influences on a person's health.\n\nExamples also include:\nPreventive health assessments/screenings, **prenatal care**, and **telehealth/telemedicine** are all examples that improve the health and well-being of populations.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udcb0 Why It Matters\n\nPracticing preventive medicine can lower costs, as 75% of the country's annual health spending goes toward chronic and largely preventable diseases. Preventive medicine helps you reduce the risk of illness **and** lower your healthcare costs.\n\n---\n\nIn summary, preventive medicine spans from vaccines and lifestyle counseling to cancer screenings, blood pressure checks, and public health policies \u2014 all aimed at keeping people healthy before illness takes hold.",
